    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Cataract, right eye operated (H25.13)</content>. An excellent result in the right eye: 82 letters with a normal pressure and a quiet eye at one month. The left cataract still limits him (VF-14 58 out of 100), and the mismatch between the eyes caused last week&apos;s fall, so the second operation goes ahead as planned. [S]<list><item>Left eye phacoemulsification in two weeks, with the same drops and checks.</item><item>New glasses after the left eye has settled.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Right wrist sprain (S63.501A)</content>. Healing; no sign of a scaphoid fracture on re-examination. [S]<list><item>Gentle strengthening; no splint needed.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic low back pain (M54.59)</content>. Pain down from 6 to 4 out of 10 with exercise therapy alone. [S]<list><item>Continue physical therapy for another four to six weeks, then a home program.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Postoperative checks: day 1, week 1 to 2, month 1</td><td>Day 1, week 1 to 2 and month 1 after each eye</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Right eye: day 1 and week 1 checks by the surgeon were normal; one-month check this morning: quiet eye, clear cornea, well-centered lens</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Best-corrected visual acuity with refraction</td><td>Before surgery and at each postoperative and routine eye examination</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Right 82 letters; left 54 letters</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Intraocular pressure</td><td>At each comprehensive and postoperative eye examination</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Right 14 mm Hg</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Functional impairment assessment (VF-14 or activity-specific: faces, signs, driving, glare)</td><td>At the preoperative evaluation</td><td>Completed today</td><td>VF-14 read aloud before the second eye: 58 out of 100; the corrective action after the missed preoperative questionnaire</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>Optical biometry and keratometry for lens implant power</td><td>Once before each eye&apos;s surgery</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Left eye biometry from the consultation is within the three-month window</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Postoperative drops: schedule, technique, taper, side effects</td><td>Each postoperative week</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Right antibiotic drops finished after a week; steroid taper finished yesterday; no missed doses</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Driving and the vision standard</td><td>Before surgery and after each eye</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Still not driving until the second eye is done</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Falls: falls since the last visit, Timed Up and Go, home hazards</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>One fall last week with a wrist sprain, from the mismatch between the eyes; glasses adjusted, second handrail fitted; Timed Up and Go 11 seconds</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Back pain intensity (0 to 10) and function</td><td>Every visit while treatment is adjusted</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4 out of 10, from 6</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Red flags: leg weakness or numbness, bladder or bowel change, fever, weight loss</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Exercise therapy (physical therapy, then a home program)</td><td>From diagnosis</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Five weeks of twice-weekly therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Pain medicines: no opioids or muscle relaxants; oral NSAID only if needed and safe</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Acetaminophen for a few days after the fall; no oral anti-inflammatories or opioids</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Pressure symptoms (swallowing, breathing, voice) and neck examination</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No pressure symptoms</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Knee pain and function; exercise; topical diclofenac use and skin</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Gel twice a day; no skin irritation</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>Find the cause of the blurred vision. Outcome by 1874-04-21: Bilateral age-related cataract; glaucoma, macular degeneration and diabetic retinopathy excluded [S]</item><item>See well enough to recognize faces, walk safely at dusk and meet the vision standard for driving. In progress; target 1874-07-21 [S]</item><item>No falls with injury. In progress; target 1875-04-07 [S]</item><item>Back pain at 3 or less out of 10 and back to a full day in the orchard. In progress; target 1874-09-29 [S]</item><item>Keep the goiter under watch without unnecessary tests. In progress; target 1876-04-21 [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Cataract in the Adult Eye Preferred Practice Pattern (2021); Noninvasive Treatments for Acute, Subacute, and Chronic Low Back Pain: A Clinical Practice Guideline From the American College of Physicians (2017); 2015 American Thyroid Association Management Guidelines for Adult Patients with Thyroid Nodules and Differentiated Thyroid Cancer (2015); 2019 American College of Rheumatology/Arthritis Foundation Guideline for the Management of Osteoarthritis of the Hand, Hip, and Knee (2019); CMID: Clinical Monitoring Intelligence Database, cataract and rheumatoid arthritis/osteoarthritis monographs (2026); Falls Prevention in Community-Dwelling Older Adults: Interventions (2024)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Left eye surgery in two weeks. Fifteen-week review five weeks after the second operation. Next scheduled visit 1874-07-21: fifteen-week review five weeks after left cataract surgery: vision with new glasses, driving, back pain and knees. [S]</paragraph></text>

